Vulkan: import dma-bufs with a dedicated allocation, into any type the fd reports

SharedTextureMemory's dma-buf path could only import memory that is
DEVICE_LOCAL, and allocated it without VkMemoryDedicatedAllocateInfo.
Both are wrong for a system-memory dma-buf on a discrete GPU -- a
dma-heap buffer, a V4L2 camera frame, a decoder's output:

  - vkGetMemoryFdPropertiesKHR only ever reports host memory types for
    such an fd, so MemoryKind::DeviceLocal finds nothing and the import
    fails with "Failed to find suitable memory type". The memory already
    exists; the type index names where it is, not where it should go.
  - NVIDIA's proprietary driver requires a dedicated allocation for the
    import but reports requires/prefersDedicatedAllocation = false, so
    detection cannot see it; without one, vkAllocateMemory returns
    VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY (and closes the fd on that failure,
    which Dawn's SystemHandle then closes again -> abort).

Select from the intersection with no required properties (the selector
still favours device-local), and always pass the dedicated info: an
imported allocation backs exactly this image, and the AHardwareBuffer
and OpaqueFD paths in the same file already allocate this way.

Measured on RTX 2080 Ti / 610.43.02 with a 576x192 R8G8B8A8 dma-heap
buffer: fd reports types {0,4}; type 0 + dedicated imports and binds,
every other combination returns -2. Supersedes upstream 2f24fba5fd,
which adds a HostCached fallback that selects type 4 and still fails.

Against Dawn v20260714.215939.
diff --git a/src/dawn/native/vulkan/SharedTextureMemoryVk.cpp b/src/dawn/native/vulkan/SharedTextureMemoryVk.cpp
